    INSERT INTO mcqs (topic_id, question, options, correct_index, explanation) VALUES
    (v_topic_id, 'A 67-year-old female on oral alendronate for 7 years presents with progressive dull, aching pain in the left thigh for 12 weeks. Radiographs reveal a transverse or short oblique fracture of the lateral cortex of the femoral diaphysis with cortical thickening (beaking) at the fracture site and no comminution. Which of the following is the most accurate diagnosis and the most appropriate next step?', '["Paget disease of bone; start calcitonin and continue the bisphosphonate at higher doses", "Pathologic fracture due to metastatic bone disease; order a bone biopsy and staging workup", "Typical osteoporotic hip fracture; continue bisphosphonate therapy and proceed with surgical fixation without medication changes", "Atypical femoral fracture (AFF); this is associated with prolonged bisphosphonate use (typically > 5 years) due to over-suppression of bone turnover; discontinue the bisphosphonate and evaluate the contralateral femur with imaging", "Stress fracture unrelated to bisphosphonate use; prescribe non-weight-bearing and continue current therapy"]'::jsonb, 3, '**Atypical femoral fractures (AFFs)** are a recognized complication of **prolonged bisphosphonate therapy (typically > 3-5 years)**. They are characterized by: (1) Location in the **subtrochanteric or femoral shaft** (NOT the femoral neck), (2) **Transverse or short oblique fracture pattern** (unlike the spiral/comminuted pattern of typical fragility fractures), (3) **Lateral cortical thickening/beaking** at the fracture site, (4) **Minimal or no comminution**, and (5) Often preceded by **prodromal thigh or groin pain** for weeks to months. AFFs are attributed to **oversuppression of bone turnover** (adynamic bone), which impairs microdamage repair. Management includes **discontinuing the bisphosphonate**, surgical fixation if complete, and **imaging the contralateral femur** (bilateral in up to 28% of cases). AFFs are a key reason for considering bisphosphonate drug holidays. (Ref: ASBMR AFF Task Force 2014; Hazzard''s Ch. 77; NEJM 2011)'),
