    (v_topic_id, 'A 69-year-old female on oral alendronate for 6 years presents with progressive dull, aching pain in the right thigh for 3 weeks. Radiographs reveal a transverse or short oblique fracture of the lateral cortex of the femoral diaphysis with cortical thickening (beaking) at the fracture site and no comminution. Which of the following is the most accurate diagnosis and the most appropriate next step?', '["Paget disease of bone; start calcitonin and continue the bisphosphonate at higher doses", "Stress fracture unrelated to bisphosphonate use; prescribe non-weight-bearing and continue current therapy", "Pathologic fracture due to metastatic bone disease; order a bone biopsy and staging workup", "Typical osteoporotic hip fracture; continue bisphosphonate therapy and proceed with surgical fixation without medication changes", "Atypical femoral fracture (AFF); this is associated with prolonged bisphosphonate use (typically > 5 years) due to over-suppression of bone turnover; discontinue the bisphosphonate and evaluate the contralateral femur with imaging"]'::jsonb, 4, '**Atypical femoral fractures (AFFs)** are a recognized complication of **prolonged bisphosphonate therapy (typically > 3-5 years)**. They are characterized by: (1) Location in the **subtrochanteric or femoral shaft** (NOT the femoral neck), (2) **Transverse or short oblique fracture pattern** (unlike the spiral/comminuted pattern of typical fragility fractures), (3) **Lateral cortical thickening/beaking** at the fracture site, (4) **Minimal or no comminution**, and (5) Often preceded by **prodromal thigh or groin pain** for weeks to months. AFFs are attributed to **oversuppression of bone turnover** (adynamic bone), which impairs microdamage repair. Management includes **discontinuing the bisphosphonate**, surgical fixation if complete, and **imaging the contralateral femur** (bilateral in up to 28% of cases). AFFs are a key reason for considering bisphosphonate drug holidays. (Ref: ASBMR AFF Task Force 2014; Hazzard''s Ch. 77; NEJM 2011)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 94-year-old postmenopausal woman has been taking oral alendronate for 5 years with good adherence. Her most recent DEXA shows a femoral neck T-score of -2.0 (improved from -2.8 at baseline). She has had no fractures during treatment. She asks about the need for continued therapy. Which of the following is the most appropriate recommendation regarding a bisphosphonate drug holiday?', '["Bisphosphonates must be continued indefinitely without interruption because bone density declines rapidly within weeks of discontinuation", "Switch to teriparatide for the drug holiday period to maintain bone density; bisphosphonates and teriparatide should always be used in alternating cycles", "Drug holidays are recommended after 2 years of therapy for all patients regardless of fracture risk", "Drug holidays are contraindicated because bisphosphonates have no residual skeletal effect after discontinuation", "After 5 years of oral bisphosphonate therapy (or 3 years of IV zoledronic acid) in patients at moderate risk, a drug holiday can be considered; reassess fracture risk periodically and resume therapy if BMD declines significantly or a fracture occurs"]'::jsonb, 4, '**Bisphosphonate drug holidays** are considered to reduce the risk of rare long-term complications (atypical femoral fractures, osteonecrosis of the jaw). Current guidelines recommend: (1) After **5 years of oral** bisphosphonate therapy (or **3 years of IV zoledronic acid**), reassess the patient''s fracture risk. (2) For patients at **moderate fracture risk** (no prior hip/vertebral fracture, T-score > -2.5), a **drug holiday** can be considered. (3) For patients at **high fracture risk** (prior vertebral/hip fracture, T-score still <= -2.5, or very high FRAX), continuing therapy for up to 10 years (oral) or 6 years (IV) is recommended before considering a holiday. (4) During a holiday, bisphosphonates retain **residual anti-resorptive activity** for months to years due to their long skeletal half-life (embedded in bone matrix). (5) **Reassess with DEXA and bone turnover markers** periodically; resume if BMD declines or fracture occurs. (Ref: ASBMR Task Force 2016; Hazzard''s Ch. 77; ACP Clinical Guidelines)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 76-year-old postmenopausal female has been taking oral alendronate for 5 years with good adherence. Her most recent DEXA shows a femoral neck T-score of -2.0 (improved from -2.8 at baseline). She has had no fractures during treatment. She asks about the need for continued therapy. Which of the following is the most appropriate recommendation regarding a bisphosphonate drug holiday?', '["Drug holidays are recommended after 2 years of therapy for all patients regardless of fracture risk", "Bisphosphonates must be continued indefinitely without interruption because bone density declines rapidly within weeks of discontinuation", "After 5 years of oral bisphosphonate therapy (or 3 years of IV zoledronic acid) in patients at moderate risk, a drug holiday can be considered; reassess fracture risk periodically and resume therapy if BMD declines significantly or a fracture occurs", "Drug holidays are contraindicated because bisphosphonates have no residual skeletal effect after discontinuation", "Switch to teriparatide for the drug holiday period to maintain bone density; bisphosphonates and teriparatide should always be used in alternating cycles"]'::jsonb, 2, '**Bisphosphonate drug holidays** are considered to reduce the risk of rare long-term complications (atypical femoral fractures, osteonecrosis of the jaw). Current guidelines recommend: (1) After **5 years of oral** bisphosphonate therapy (or **3 years of IV zoledronic acid**), reassess the patient''s fracture risk. (2) For patients at **moderate fracture risk** (no prior hip/vertebral fracture, T-score > -2.5), a **drug holiday** can be considered. (3) For patients at **high fracture risk** (prior vertebral/hip fracture, T-score still <= -2.5, or very high FRAX), continuing therapy for up to 10 years (oral) or 6 years (IV) is recommended before considering a holiday. (4) During a holiday, bisphosphonates retain **residual anti-resorptive activity** for months to years due to their long skeletal half-life (embedded in bone matrix). (5) **Reassess with DEXA and bone turnover markers** periodically; resume if BMD declines or fracture occurs. (Ref: ASBMR Task Force 2016; Hazzard''s Ch. 77; ACP Clinical Guidelines)'),
