    (v_topic_id, 'A 95-year-old postmenopausal woman has been taking oral alendronate for 8 years with good adherence. Her most recent DEXA shows a femoral neck T-score of -2.0 (improved from -2.8 at baseline). She has had no fractures during treatment. She asks about the need for continued therapy. Which of the following is the most appropriate recommendation regarding a bisphosphonate drug holiday?', '["After 5 years of oral bisphosphonate therapy (or 3 years of IV zoledronic acid) in patients at moderate risk, a drug holiday can be considered; reassess fracture risk periodically and resume therapy if BMD declines significantly or a fracture occurs", "Bisphosphonates must be continued indefinitely without interruption because bone density declines rapidly within weeks of discontinuation", "Drug holidays are recommended after 2 years of therapy for all patients regardless of fracture risk", "Switch to teriparatide for the drug holiday period to maintain bone density; bisphosphonates and teriparatide should always be used in alternating cycles", "Drug holidays are contraindicated because bisphosphonates have no residual skeletal effect after discontinuation"]'::jsonb, 0, '**Bisphosphonate drug holidays** are considered to reduce the risk of rare long-term complications (atypical femoral fractures, osteonecrosis of the jaw). Current guidelines recommend: (1) After **5 years of oral** bisphosphonate therapy (or **3 years of IV zoledronic acid**), reassess the patient''s fracture risk. (2) For patients at **moderate fracture risk** (no prior hip/vertebral fracture, T-score > -2.5), a **drug holiday** can be considered. (3) For patients at **high fracture risk** (prior vertebral/hip fracture, T-score still <= -2.5, or very high FRAX), continuing therapy for up to 10 years (oral) or 6 years (IV) is recommended before considering a holiday. (4) During a holiday, bisphosphonates retain **residual anti-resorptive activity** for months to years due to their long skeletal half-life (embedded in bone matrix). (5) **Reassess with DEXA and bone turnover markers** periodically; resume if BMD declines or fracture occurs. (Ref: ASBMR Task Force 2016; Hazzard''s Ch. 77; ACP Clinical Guidelines)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 68-year-old postmenopausal woman has been taking oral alendronate for 5 years with good adherence. Her most recent DEXA shows a femoral neck T-score of -2.0 (improved from -2.8 at baseline). She has had no fractures during treatment. She asks about the need for continued therapy. Which of the following is the most appropriate recommendation regarding a bisphosphonate drug holiday?', '["Switch to teriparatide for the drug holiday period to maintain bone density; bisphosphonates and teriparatide should always be used in alternating cycles", "Drug holidays are recommended after 2 years of therapy for all patients regardless of fracture risk", "Drug holidays are contraindicated because bisphosphonates have no residual skeletal effect after discontinuation", "After 5 years of oral bisphosphonate therapy (or 3 years of IV zoledronic acid) in patients at moderate risk, a drug holiday can be considered; reassess fracture risk periodically and resume therapy if BMD declines significantly or a fracture occurs", "Bisphosphonates must be continued indefinitely without interruption because bone density declines rapidly within weeks of discontinuation"]'::jsonb, 3, '**Bisphosphonate drug holidays** are considered to reduce the risk of rare long-term complications (atypical femoral fractures, osteonecrosis of the jaw). Current guidelines recommend: (1) After **5 years of oral** bisphosphonate therapy (or **3 years of IV zoledronic acid**), reassess the patient''s fracture risk. (2) For patients at **moderate fracture risk** (no prior hip/vertebral fracture, T-score > -2.5), a **drug holiday** can be considered. (3) For patients at **high fracture risk** (prior vertebral/hip fracture, T-score still <= -2.5, or very high FRAX), continuing therapy for up to 10 years (oral) or 6 years (IV) is recommended before considering a holiday. (4) During a holiday, bisphosphonates retain **residual anti-resorptive activity** for months to years due to their long skeletal hal