lf-life (embedded in bone matrix). (5) **Reassess with DEXA and bone turnover markers** periodically; resume if BMD declines or fracture occurs. (Ref: ASBMR Task Force 2016; Hazzard''s Ch. 77; ACP Clinical Guidelines)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 95-year-old postmenopausal woman has been taking oral alendronate for 5 years with good adherence. Her most recent DEXA shows a femoral neck T-score of -2.0 (improved from -2.8 at baseline). She has had no fractures during treatment. She asks about the need for continued therapy. Which of the following is the most appropriate recommendation regarding a bisphosphonate drug holiday?', '["After 5 years of oral bisphosphonate therapy (or 3 years of IV zoledronic acid) in patients at moderate risk, a drug holiday can be considered; reassess fracture risk periodically and resume therapy if BMD declines significantly or a fracture occurs", "Drug holidays are recommended after 2 years of therapy for all patients regardless of fracture risk", "Bisphosphonates must be continued indefinitely without interruption because bone density declines rapidly within weeks of discontinuation", "Drug holidays are contraindicated because bisphosphonates have no residual skeletal effect after discontinuation", "Switch to teriparatide for the drug holiday period to maintain bone density; bisphosphonates and teriparatide should always be used in alternating cycles"]'::jsonb, 0, '**Bisphosphonate drug holidays** are considered to reduce the risk of rare long-term complications (atypical femoral fractures, osteonecrosis of the jaw). Current guidelines recommend: (1) After **5 years of oral** bisphosphonate therapy (or **3 years of IV zoledronic acid**), reassess the patient''s fracture risk. (2) For patients at **moderate fracture risk** (no prior hip/vertebral fracture, T-score > -2.5), a **drug holiday** can be considered. (3) For patients at **high fracture risk** (prior vertebral/hip fracture, T-score still <= -2.5, or very high FRAX), continuing therapy for up to 10 years (oral) or 6 years (IV) is recommended before considering a holiday. (4) During a holiday, bisphosphonates retain **residual anti-resorptive activity** for months to years due to their long skeletal half-life (embedded in bone matrix). (5) **Reassess with DEXA and bone turnover markers** periodically; resume if BMD declines or fracture occurs. (Ref: ASBMR Task Force 2016; Hazzard''s Ch. 77; ACP Clinical Guidelines)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 88-year-old woman underwent surgical repair of a low-trauma hip fracture 20 days ago. Prior to the fracture, she was not on any osteoporosis treatment and had never had a DEXA scan. Her serum calcium is 8.9 mg/dL and 25-hydroxyvitamin D is 26 ng/mL. Which of the following is the most appropriate osteoporosis management strategy?', '["No osteoporosis treatment is indicated because the fracture was caused by the fall, not by weak bones", "Start oral alendronate 70 mg weekly immediately on postoperative day 1 before the patient can sit upright", "Defer all treatment until a DEXA scan confirms a T-score <= -2.5, as fractures alone do not justify pharmacotherapy", "Initiate zoledronic acid 5 mg IV within 2-12 weeks post-fracture; vitamin D is adequate (>= 20 ng/mL); the HORIZON Recurrent Fracture Trial showed this reduces recurrent fractures by 35% and mortality by 28% after hip fracture; a DEXA scan should be obtained but should NOT delay treatment", "Wait 12 months after the fracture before considering any osteoporosis treatment to allow for bone healing"]'::jsonb, 3, 'After a **low-trauma hip fracture**, pharmacologic osteoporosis treatment should be **initiated during or shortly after the hospitalization** (within 2-12 weeks post-fracture), as this population has extremely high refracture and mortality risk (up to 20-30% 1-year mortality after hip fracture). **Zoledronic acid 5 mg IV** is the preferred agent because it is given as a once-yearly infusion (ensuring adherence in a population with high pill burden and swallowing difficulties). The **HORIZON Recurrent Fracture Trial** demonstrated that zoledronic acid administered within 90 days of hip fracture repair reduced recurrent fractures by 35% and mortality by 28%. **Prerequisite**: Correct **vitamin D deficiency** (25(OH)D should be >= 20 ng/mL) and ensure **adequate calcium** before infusion to prevent hypocalcemia. A DEXA scan can be performed later but should NOT delay treatment initiation after a fragility fracture. (Ref: HORIZON-RFT, NEJM 2007; Hazzard''s Ch. 77; NOF Guidelines)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 83-year-old female underwent surgical repair of a low-trauma hip fracture 10 days ago. Prior to the fracture, she was not on any osteoporosis treatment and had never had a DEXA scan. Her serum calcium is 9.1 mg/dL and 25-hydroxyvitamin D is 21 ng/mL. Which of the following is the most appropriate osteoporosis management strategy?', '["Defer all treatment until a DEXA scan confirms a T-score <= -2.5, as fractures alone do not justify pharmacotherapy", "Wait 12 months after the fracture before considering any osteoporosis treatment to allow for bone healing", "Start oral alendronate 70 mg weekly immediately on postoperative day 1 before the patient can sit upright", "Initiate zoledronic acid 5 mg IV within 2-12 weeks post-fracture; vitamin D is adequate (>= 20 ng/mL); the HORIZON Recurrent Fracture Trial showed this reduces recurrent fractures by 35% and mortality by 28% after hip fracture; a DEXA scan should be obtained but should NOT delay treatment", "No osteoporosis treatment is indicated because the fracture was caused by the fall, not by weak bones"]'::jsonb, 3, 'After a **low-trauma hip fracture**, pharmacologic osteoporosis treatment should be **initiated during or shortly after the hospitalization** (within 2-12 weeks post-fracture), as this population has extremely high refracture and mortality risk (up to 20-30% 1-year mortality after hip fracture). **Zoledronic acid 5 mg IV** is the preferred agent because it is given as a once-yearly infusion (ensuring adherence in a population with high pill burden and swallowing difficulties). The **HORIZON Recurrent Fracture Trial** demonstrated that zoledronic acid administered within 90 days of hip fracture repair reduced recurrent fractures by 35% and mortality by 28%. **Prerequisite**: Correct **vitamin D deficiency** (25(OH)D should be >= 20 ng/mL) and ensure **adequate calcium** before infusion to prevent hypocalcemia. A DEXA scan can be performed later but should NOT delay treatment initiation after a fragility fracture. (Ref: HORIZON-RFT, NEJM 2007; Hazzard''s Ch. 77; NOF Guidelines)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 69-year-old female underwent surgical repair of a low-trauma hip fracture 8 days ago. Prior to the fracture, she was not on any osteoporosis treatment and had never had a DEXA scan. Her serum calcium is 8.4 mg/dL and 25-hydroxyvitamin D is 23 ng/mL. Which of the following is the most appropriate osteoporosis management strategy?', '["Initiate zoledronic acid 5 mg IV within 2-12 weeks post-fracture; vitamin D is adequate (>= 20 ng/mL); the HORIZON Recurrent Fracture Trial showed this reduces recurrent fractures by 35% and mortality by 28% after hip fracture; a DEXA scan should be obtained but should NOT delay treatment", "Defer all treatment until a DEXA scan confirms a T-score <= -2.5, as fractures alone do not justify pharmacotherapy", "Start oral alendronate 70 mg weekly immediately on postoperative day 1 before the patient can sit upright", "Wait 12 months after the fracture before considering any osteoporosis treatment to allow for bone healing", "No osteoporosis treatment is indicated because the fracture was caused by the fall, not by weak bones"]'::jsonb, 0, 'After a **low-trauma hip fracture**, pharmacologic osteoporosis treatment should be **initiated during or shortly after the hospitalization** (within 2-12 weeks post-fracture), as this population has extremely high refracture and mortality risk (up to 20-30% 1-year mortality after hip fracture). **Zoledronic acid 5 mg IV** is the preferred agent because it is given as a once-yearly infusion (ensuring adherence in a population with high pill burden and swallowing difficulties). The **HORIZON Recurrent Fracture Trial** demonstrated that zoledronic acid administered within 90 days of hip fracture repair reduced recurrent fractures by 35% and mortality by 28%. **Prerequisite**: Correct **vitamin D deficiency** (25(OH)D should be >= 20 ng/mL) and ensure **adequate calcium** before infusion to prevent hypocalcemia. A DEXA scan can be performed later but should NOT delay treatment initiation after a fragility fracture. (Ref: HORIZON-RFT, NEJM 2007; Hazzard''s Ch. 77; NOF Guidelines)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 66-year-old woman underwent surgical repair of a low-trauma hip fracture 17 days ago. Prior to the fracture, she was not on any osteoporosis treatment and had never had a DEXA scan. Her serum calcium is 8.8 mg/dL and 25-hydroxyvitamin D is 18 ng/mL. Which of the following is the most appropriate osteoporosis management strategy?', '["First correct the vitamin D deficiency (25(OH)D 18 ng/mL < 20 ng/mL) with high-dose cholecalciferol loading, then initiate zoledronic acid 5 mg IV within 2-12 weeks post-fracture; vitamin D must be repleted before bisphosphonate infusion to prevent severe hypocalcemia", "Prescribe calcitonin nasal spray as the sole treatment because it is the only agent approved for post-fracture management", "No osteoporosis treatment is needed because the vitamin D deficiency alone caused the fracture, and repletion will be sufficient", "Defer all treatment until the patient is fully rehabilitated (typically 6-12 months post-fracture)", "Start zoledronic acid immediately without correcting vitamin D because the fracture risk is too high to delay treatment"]'::jsonb, 0, 'After a **low-trauma hip fracture**, pharmacologic osteoporosis treatment should be **initiated during or shortly after the hospitalization** (within 2-12 weeks post-fracture), as this population has extremely high refracture and mortality risk (up to 20-30% 1-year mortality after hip fracture). **Zoledronic acid 5 mg IV** is the preferred agent because it is given as a once-yearly infusion (ensuring adherence in a population with high pill burden and swallowing difficulties). The **HORIZON Recurrent Fracture Trial** demonstrated that zoledronic acid administered within 90 days of hip fracture repair reduced recurrent fractures by 35% and mortality by 28%. **Prerequisite**: Correct **vitamin D deficiency** (25(OH)D should be >= 20 ng/mL) and ensure **adequate calcium** before infusion to prevent hypocalcemia. A DEXA scan can be performed later but should NOT delay treatment initiation after a fragility fracture. (Ref: HORIZON-RFT, NEJM 2007; Hazzard''s Ch. 77; NOF Guidelines)'),
