n after a fragility fracture. (Ref: HORIZON-RFT, NEJM 2007; Hazzard''s Ch. 77; NOF Guidelines)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 87-year-old female underwent surgical repair of a low-trauma hip fracture 8 days ago. Prior to the fracture, she was not on any osteoporosis treatment and had never had a DEXA scan. Her serum calcium is 9.4 mg/dL and 25-hydroxyvitamin D is 30 ng/mL. Which of the following is the most appropriate osteoporosis management strategy?', '["Wait 12 months after the fracture before considering any osteoporosis treatment to allow for bone healing", "Start oral alendronate 70 mg weekly immediately on postoperative day 1 before the patient can sit upright", "Defer all treatment until a DEXA scan confirms a T-score <= -2.5, as fractures alone do not justify pharmacotherapy", "Initiate zoledronic acid 5 mg IV within 2-12 weeks post-fracture; vitamin D is adequate (>= 20 ng/mL); the HORIZON Recurrent Fracture Trial showed this reduces recurrent fractures by 35% and mortality by 28% after hip fracture; a DEXA scan should be obtained but should NOT delay treatment", "No osteoporosis treatment is indicated because the fracture was caused by the fall, not by weak bones"]'::jsonb, 3, 'After a **low-trauma hip fracture**, pharmacologic osteoporosis treatment should be **initiated during or shortly after the hospitalization** (within 2-12 weeks post-fracture), as this population has extremely high refracture and mortality risk (up to 20-30% 1-year mortality after hip fracture). **Zoledronic acid 5 mg IV** is the preferred agent because it is given as a once-yearly infusion (ensuring adherence in a population with high pill burden and swallowing difficulties). The **HORIZON Recurrent Fracture Trial** demonstrated that zoledronic acid administered within 90 days of hip fracture repair reduced recurrent fractures by 35% and mortality by 28%. **Prerequisite**: Correct **vitamin D deficiency** (25(OH)D should be >= 20 ng/mL) and ensure **adequate calcium** before infusion to prevent hypocalcemia. A DEXA scan can be performed later but should NOT delay treatment initiation after a fragility fracture. (Ref: HORIZON-RFT, NEJM 2007; Hazzard''s Ch. 77; NOF Guidelines)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 93-year-old postmenopausal female is diagnosed with osteoporosis (T-score -3.0 at the lumbar spine). She has a history of gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD) with Barrett esophagus and cannot tolerate oral medications that require prolonged upright positioning. She is otherwise healthy. Which of the following is the most appropriate first-line bisphosphonate for this patient?', '["Oral risedronate 35 mg weekly, as it has no risk of esophageal side effects unlike alendronate", "Oral ibandronate 150 mg monthly, as monthly dosing eliminates the esophageal risk compared to weekly dosing", "Defer all bisphosphonate therapy until the Barrett esophagus resolves spontaneously", "Zoledronic acid 5 mg IV once yearly; IV administration bypasses the esophagus, avoiding the risk of esophageal injury in patients with pre-existing esophageal disorders", "Oral alendronate 70 mg weekly with careful upright positioning for 60 minutes instead of the standard 30 minutes"]'::jsonb, 3, '**Oral bisphosphonates are contraindicated** in patients with **esophageal abnormalities that delay esophageal emptying** (e.g., strictures, achalasia) and in patients with **inability to stand or sit upright for at least 30 minutes**. Barrett esophagus is a relative contraindication due to the pre-existing esophageal mucosal injury. In these patients, **IV zoledronic acid 5 mg once yearly** is the preferred first-line bisphosphonate because it bypasses the gastrointestinal tract entirely. IV ibandronate (3 mg quarterly) is an alternative but has less evidence for hip fracture reduction compared to zoledronic acid. All oral bisphosphonates (alendronate, risedronate, ibandronate) carry the same class-wide risk of esophageal injury. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 77; FDA Label; NOF Guidelines)'),
