    (v_topic_id, 'A 82-year-old postmenopausal woman with osteoporosis is started on denosumab 60 mg subcutaneously every 6 months after failing to tolerate oral bisphosphonates and having a contraindication to IV bisphosphonates due to severe renal insufficiency (eGFR 25 mL/min). Which of the following best describes the mechanism of denosumab and the most important counseling point?', '["Denosumab is a fully human monoclonal antibody against RANKL that prevents osteoclast differentiation and activation; discontinuation carries a risk of rapid rebound bone loss and vertebral fractures, so patients must transition to an alternative anti-resorptive before stopping", "Denosumab is administered orally once monthly and has no effect on bone turnover markers", "Denosumab directly inhibits farnesyl pyrophosphate synthase in osteoclasts, identical to the bisphosphonate mechanism", "Denosumab has a long skeletal half-life like bisphosphonates, so a drug holiday can be safely taken without rebound effects", "Denosumab stimulates osteoblast activity through the Wnt/sclerostin pathway, making it a purely anabolic agent"]'::jsonb, 0, '**Denosumab** is a **fully human monoclonal antibody against RANKL** (Receptor Activator of Nuclear factor Kappa-B Ligand). RANKL is essential for osteoclast differentiation, activation, and survival. By binding and neutralizing RANKL, denosumab **prevents osteoclast formation and function**, reducing bone resorption. It is administered as a **60 mg subcutaneous injection every 6 months**. **Critical counseling**: Unlike bisphosphonates, denosumab has **NO residual skeletal effect** after discontinuation. Stopping denosumab causes **rapid rebound bone turnover**, with BMD declining to or below pre-treatment levels within 12-18 months and a significant increase in **vertebral compression fractures**. Therefore, if denosumab is discontinued, the patient must be **transitioned to a bisphosphonate** (e.g., alendronate or zoledronic acid) to prevent rebound bone loss. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 77; NEJM 2017; Bone 2011; FREEDOM Extension Trial)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 80-year-old postmenopausal woman with osteoporosis is started on denosumab 60 mg subcutaneously every 6 months after failing to tolerate oral bisphosphonates and having a contraindication to IV bisphosphonates due to chronic kidney disease stage 4 (eGFR 22 mL/min). Which of the following best describes the mechanism of denosumab and the most important counseling point?', '["Denosumab stimulates osteoblast activity through the Wnt/sclerostin pathway, making it a purely anabolic agent", "Denosumab is administered orally once monthly and has no effect on bone turnover markers", "Denosumab directly inhibits farnesyl pyrophosphate synthase in osteoclasts, identical to the bisphosphonate mechanism", "Denosumab has a long skeletal half-life like bisphosphonates, so a drug holiday can be safely taken without rebound effects", "Denosumab is a fully human monoclonal antibody against RANKL that prevents osteoclast differentiation and activation; discontinuation carries a risk of rapid rebound bone loss and vertebral fractures, so patients must transition to an alternative anti-resorptive before stopping"]'::jsonb, 4, '**Denosumab** is a **fully human monoclonal antibody against RANKL** (Receptor Activator of Nuclear factor Kappa-B Ligand). RANKL is essential for osteoclast differentiation, activation, and survival. By binding and neutralizing RANKL, denosumab **prevents osteoclast formation and function**, reducing bone resorption. It is administered as a **60 mg subcutaneous injection every 6 months**. **Critical counseling**: Unlike bisphosphonates, denosumab has **NO residual skeletal effect** after discontinuation. Stopping denosumab causes **rapid rebound bone turnover**, with BMD declining to or below pre-treatment levels within 12-18 months and a significant increase in **vertebral compression fractures**. Therefore, if denosumab is discontinued, the patient must be **transitioned to a bisphosphonate** (e.g., alendronate or zoledronic acid) to prevent rebound bone loss. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 77; NEJM 2017; Bone 2011; FREEDOM Extension Trial)'),
