    (v_topic_id, 'A 79-year-old postmenopausal female with osteoporosis is started on denosumab 60 mg subcutaneously every 6 months after failing to tolerate oral bisphosphonates and having a contraindication to IV bisphosphonates due to chronic kidney disease stage 4 (eGFR 22 mL/min). Which of the following best describes the mechanism of denosumab and the most important counseling point?', '["Denosumab has a long skeletal half-life like bisphosphonates, so a drug holiday can be safely taken without rebound effects", "Denosumab directly inhibits farnesyl pyrophosphate synthase in osteoclasts, identical to the bisphosphonate mechanism", "Denosumab is a fully human monoclonal antibody against RANKL that prevents osteoclast differentiation and activation; discontinuation carries a risk of rapid rebound bone loss and vertebral fractures, so patients must transition to an alternative anti-resorptive before stopping", "Denosumab stimulates osteoblast activity through the Wnt/sclerostin pathway, making it a purely anabolic agent", "Denosumab is administered orally once monthly and has no effect on bone turnover markers"]'::jsonb, 2, '**Denosumab** is a **fully human monoclonal antibody against RANKL** (Receptor Activator of Nuclear factor Kappa-B Ligand). RANKL is essential for osteoclast differentiation, activation, and survival. By binding and neutralizing RANKL, denosumab **prevents osteoclast formation and function**, reducing bone resorption. It is administered as a **60 mg subcutaneous injection every 6 months**. **Critical counseling**: Unlike bisphosphonates, denosumab has **NO residual skeletal effect** after discontinuation. Stopping denosumab causes **rapid rebound bone turnover**, with BMD declining to or below pre-treatment levels within 12-18 months and a significant increase in **vertebral compression fractures**. Therefore, if denosumab is discontinued, the patient must be **transitioned to a bisphosphonate** (e.g., alendronate or zoledronic acid) to prevent rebound bone loss. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 77; NEJM 2017; Bone 2011; FREEDOM Extension Trial)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 88-year-old postmenopausal female with osteoporosis is started on denosumab 60 mg subcutaneously every 6 months after failing to tolerate oral bisphosphonates and having a contraindication to IV bisphosphonates due to chronic kidney disease stage 4 (eGFR 22 mL/min). Which of the following best describes the mechanism of denosumab and the most important counseling point?', '["Denosumab is a fully human monoclonal antibody against RANKL that prevents osteoclast differentiation and activation; discontinuation carries a risk of rapid rebound bone loss and vertebral fractures, so patients must transition to an alternative anti-resorptive before stopping", "Denosumab has a long skeletal half-life like bisphosphonates, so a drug holiday can be safely taken without rebound effects", "Denosumab is administered orally once monthly and has no effect on bone turnover markers", "Denosumab directly inhibits farnesyl pyrophosphate synthase in osteoclasts, identical to the bisphosphonate mechanism", "Denosumab stimulates osteoblast activity through the Wnt/sclerostin pathway, making it a purely anabolic agent"]'::jsonb, 0, '**Denosumab** is a **fully human monoclonal antibody against RANKL** (Receptor Activator of Nuclear factor Kappa-B Ligand). RANKL is essential for osteoclast differentiation, activation, and survival. By binding and neutralizing RANKL, denosumab **prevents osteoclast formation and function**, reducing bone resorption. It is administered as a **60 mg subcutaneous injection every 6 months**. **Critical counseling**: Unlike bisphosphonates, denosumab has **NO residual skeletal effect** after discontinuation. Stopping denosumab causes **rapid rebound bone turnover**, with BMD declining to or below pre-treatment levels within 12-18 months and a significant increase in **vertebral compression fractures**. Therefore, if denosumab is discontinued, the patient must be **transitioned to a bisphosphonate** (e.g., alendronate or zoledronic acid) to prevent rebound bone loss. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 77; NEJM 2017; Bone 2011; FREEDOM Extension Trial)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 67-year-old postmenopausal woman with severe osteoporosis (T-score -3.8 at the lumbar spine) and 4 vertebral compression fractures despite 7 years of bisphosphonate therapy is being considered for teriparatide (recombinant PTH 1-34). Which of the following best describes the indication, mechanism, and limitations of teriparatide?', '["Teriparatide works by stimulating calcitonin secretion from the thyroid gland to directly suppress osteoclast activity", "Teriparatide is an anti-resorptive agent that inhibits osteoclasts through the RANKL pathway; it can be used indefinitely without a time limit", "Teriparatide is an anabolic agent that stimulates osteoblast-mediated new bone formation when given intermittently (daily subcutaneous injection); it is indicated for severe osteoporosis with fractures or treatment failure; maximum treatment duration is 2 years, followed by consolidation with an anti-resorptive agent", "Teriparatide is indicated as first-line therapy for mild osteopenia (T-score -1.0 to -1.5) before trying bisphosphonates", "Teriparatide is administered as a once-yearly intravenous infusion and has no duration restrictions"]'::jsonb, 2, '**Teriparatide** (recombinant human PTH 1-34) is the first FDA-approved **anabolic (bone-forming) agent** for osteoporosis. When given as **intermittent daily subcutaneous injections (20 mcg/day)**, it preferentially stimulates **osteoblast activity and new bone formation** (unlike the continuous PTH elevation in hyperparathyroidism, which causes resorption). Indications include **severe osteoporosis** (very low T-scores), **multiple fractures**, **fracture despite bisphosphonate therapy** (treatment failure), and **glucocorticoid-induced osteoporosis**. The maximum treatment duration is **2 years** (due to a theoretical risk of osteosarcoma observed in rat studies at high doses). After completing teriparatide therapy, patients MUST be transitioned to an **anti-resorptive agent** (bisphosphonate or denosumab) to **consolidate** the bone gains; without this step, newly formed bone is rapidly resorbed. **Contraindications**: Paget disease, unexplained elevated alkaline phosphatase, prior bone radiation, open epiphyses, pre-existing hypercalcemia, and bone metastases. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 77; NEJM 2001; NOF Guidelines)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 87-year-old postmenopausal female with severe osteoporosis (T-score -3.8 at the lumbar spine) and 2 vertebral compression fractures despite 4 years of bisphosphonate therapy is being considered for teriparatide (recombinant PTH 1-34). Which of the following best describes the indication, mechanism, and limitations of teriparatide?', '["Teriparatide is administered as a once-yearly intravenous infusion and has no duration restrictions", "Teriparatide works by stimulating calcitonin secretion from the thyroid gland to directly suppress osteoclast activity", "Teriparatide is an anti-resorptive agent that inhibits osteoclasts through the RANKL pathway; it can be used indefinitely without a time limit", "Teriparatide is an anabolic agent that stimulates osteoblast-mediated new bone formation when given intermittently (daily subcutaneous injection); it is indicated for severe osteoporosis with fractures or treatment failure; maximum treatment duration is 2 years, followed by consolidation with an anti-resorptive agent", "Teriparatide is indicated as first-line therapy for mild osteopenia (T-score -1.0 to -1.5) before trying bisphosphonates"]'::jsonb, 3, '**Teriparatide** (recombinant human PTH 1-34) is the first FDA-approved **anabolic (bone-forming) agent** for osteoporosis. When given as **intermittent daily subcutaneous injections (20 mcg/day)**, it preferentially stimulates **osteoblast activity and new bone formation** (unlike the continuous PTH elevation in hyperparathyroidism, which causes resorption). Indications include **severe osteoporosis** (very low T-scores), **multiple fractures**, **fracture despite bisphosphonate therapy** (treatment failure), and **glucocorticoid-induced osteoporosis**. The maximum treatment duration is **2 years** (due to a theoretical risk of osteosarcoma observed in rat studies at high doses). After completing teriparatide therapy, patients MUST be transitioned to an **anti-resorptive agent** (bisphosphonate or den