    (v_topic_id, 'A 93-year-old postmenopausal woman with severe osteoporosis (T-score -3.8 at the lumbar spine) and 4 vertebral compression fractures despite 4 years of bisphosphonate therapy is being considered for teriparatide (recombinant PTH 1-34). Which of the following best describes the indication, mechanism, and limitations of teriparatide?', '["Teriparatide works by stimulating calcitonin secretion from the thyroid gland to directly suppress osteoclast activity", "Teriparatide is administered as a once-yearly intravenous infusion and has no duration restrictions", "Teriparatide is indicated as first-line therapy for mild osteopenia (T-score -1.0 to -1.5) before trying bisphosphonates", "Teriparatide is an anti-resorptive agent that inhibits osteoclasts through the RANKL pathway; it can be used indefinitely without a time limit", "Teriparatide is an anabolic agent that stimulates osteoblast-mediated new bone formation when given intermittently (daily subcutaneous injection); it is indicated for severe osteoporosis with fractures or treatment failure; maximum treatment duration is 2 years, followed by consolidation with an anti-resorptive agent"]'::jsonb, 4, '**Teriparatide** (recombinant human PTH 1-34) is the first FDA-approved **anabolic (bone-forming) agent** for osteoporosis. When given as **intermittent daily subcutaneous injections (20 mcg/day)**, it preferentially stimulates **osteoblast activity and new bone formation** (unlike the continuous PTH elevation in hyperparathyroidism, which causes resorption). Indications include **severe osteoporosis** (very low T-scores), **multiple fractures**, **fracture despite bisphosphonate therapy** (treatment failure), and **glucocorticoid-induced osteoporosis**. The maximum treatment duration is **2 years** (due to a theoretical risk of osteosarcoma observed in rat studies at high doses). After completing teriparatide therapy, patients MUST be transitioned to an **anti-resorptive agent** (bisphosphonate or deno