    (v_topic_id, 'A 66-year-old postmenopausal female with severe osteoporosis (T-score -3.8 at the lumbar spine) and 2 vertebral compression fractures despite 6 years of bisphosphonate therapy is being considered for teriparatide (recombinant PTH 1-34). Which of the following best describes the indication, mechanism, and limitations of teriparatide?', '["Teriparatide is indicated as first-line therapy for mild osteopenia (T-score -1.0 to -1.5) before trying bisphosphonates", "Teriparatide works by stimulating calcitonin secretion from the thyroid gland to directly suppress osteoclast activity", "Teriparatide is an anti-resorptive agent that inhibits osteoclasts through the RANKL pathway; it can be used indefinitely without a time limit", "Teriparatide is administered as a once-yearly intravenous infusion and has no duration restrictions", "Teriparatide is an anabolic agent that stimulates osteoblast-mediated new bone formation when given intermittently (daily subcutaneous injection); it is indicated for severe osteoporosis with fractures or treatment failure; maximum treatment duration is 2 years, followed by consolidation with an anti-resorptive agent"]'::jsonb, 4, '**Teriparatide** (recombinant human PTH 1-34) is the first FDA-approved **anabolic (bone-forming) agent** for osteoporosis. When given as **intermittent daily subcutaneous injections (20 mcg/day)**, it preferentially stimulates **osteoblast activity and new bone formation** (unlike the continuous PTH elevation in hyperparathyroidism, which causes resorption). Indications include **severe osteoporosis** (very low T-scores), **multiple fractures**, **fracture despite bisphosphonate therapy** (treatment failure), and **glucocorticoid-induced osteoporosis**. The maximum treatment duration is **2 years** (due to a theoretical risk of osteosarcoma observed in rat studies at high doses). After completing teriparatide therapy, patients MUST be transitioned to an **anti-resorptive agent** (bisphosphonate or denosumab) to **consolidate** the bone gains; without this step, newly formed bone is rapidly resorbed. **Contraindications**: Paget disease, unexplained elevated alkaline phosphatase, prior bone radiation, open epiphyses, pre-existing hypercalcemia, and bone metastases. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 77; NEJM 2001; NOF Guidelines)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 75-year-old postmenopausal female with osteoporosis (T-score -2.7 at the lumbar spine) also has a strong family history of invasive breast cancer. She has no personal history of venous thromboembolism. She asks about raloxifene as a treatment option. Which of the following best describes the role of raloxifene in osteoporosis management?', '["Raloxifene is an estrogen agonist on both bone and breast tissue, increasing breast cancer risk", "Raloxifene is the first-line agent for osteoporosis in all postmenopausal women, regardless of breast cancer history", "Raloxifene reduces both vertebral and hip fracture risk equally and has no effect on breast cancer incidence", "Raloxifene is a selective estrogen receptor modulator (SERM) that acts as an estrogen agonist on bone (reducing vertebral fracture risk) and an estrogen antagonist on breast tissue (reducing invasive breast cancer risk); it does NOT reduce hip fracture risk and increases the risk of venous thromboembolism and hot flashes", "Raloxifene acts as an estrogen antagonist on bone, reducing bone density, and is used solely for breast cancer chemoprevention"]'::jsonb, 3, '**Raloxifene** is a **selective estrogen receptor modulator (SERM)** with tissue-specific effects: (1) **Estrogen agonist on bone** - reduces bone resorption and decreases the risk of **vertebral fractures by ~30%**. (2) **Estrogen antagonist on breast tissue** - reduces the risk of **invasive ER-positive breast cancer by ~65%**. (3) **Does NOT reduce hip or non-vertebral fracture risk** (unlike bisphosphonates, denosumab, or teriparatide). **Adverse effects** include increased risk of **venous thromboembolism (DVT/PE)**, **hot flashes**, and **leg cramps**. Raloxifene is NOT first-line for osteoporosis (bisphosphonates are superior) but is a reasonable option for postmenopausal women at high breast cancer risk who also have osteoporosis, provided they have no history or risk factors for VTE. It is **contraindicated** in women with active or past VTE. (Ref: MORE Trial, JAMA 1999; Hazzard''s Ch. 77; First Aid Step 2 CK)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 80-year-old postmenopausal woman with osteoporosis (T-score -2.7 at the lumbar spine) also has a strong family history of invasive breast cancer. She has no personal history of venous thromboembolism. She asks about raloxifene as a treatment option. Which of the following best describes the role of raloxifene in osteoporosis management?', '["Raloxifene acts as an estrogen antagonist on bone, reducing bone density, and is used solely for breast cancer chemoprevention", "Raloxifene is the first-line agent for osteoporosis in all postmenopausal women, regardless of breast cancer history", "Raloxifene reduces both vertebral and hip fracture risk equally and has no effect on breast cancer incidence", "Raloxifene is an estrogen agonist on both bone and breast tissue, increasing breast cancer risk", "Raloxifene is a selective estrogen receptor modulator (SERM) that acts as an estrogen agonist on bone (reducing vertebral fracture risk) and an estrogen antagonist on breast tissue (reducing invasive breast cancer risk); it does NOT reduce hip fracture risk and increases the risk of venous thromboembolism and hot flashes"]'::jsonb, 4, '**Raloxifene** is a **selective estrogen receptor modulator (SERM)** with tissue-specific effects: (1) **Estrogen agonist on bone** - reduces bone resorption and decreases the risk of **vertebral fractures by ~30%**. (2) **Estrogen antagonist on breast tissue** - reduces the risk of **invasive ER-positive breast cancer by ~65%**. (3) **Does NOT reduce hip or non-vertebral fracture risk** (unlike bisphosphonates, denosumab, or teriparatide). **Adverse effects** include increased risk of **venous thromboembolism (DVT/PE)**, **hot flashes**, and **leg cramps**. Raloxifene is NOT first-line for osteoporosis (bisphosphonates are superior) but is a reasonable option for postmenopausal women at high breast cancer risk who also have osteoporosis, provided they have no history or risk factors for VTE. It is **contraindicated** in women with active or past VTE. (Ref: MORE Trial, JAMA 1999; Hazzard''s Ch. 77; First Aid Step 2 CK)'),
