en with active or past VTE. (Ref: MORE Trial, JAMA 1999; Hazzard''s Ch. 77; First Aid Step 2 CK)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 87-year-old postmenopausal female with osteoporosis (T-score -2.7 at the lumbar spine) also has a strong family history of invasive breast cancer. She has no personal history of venous thromboembolism. She asks about raloxifene as a treatment option. Which of the following best describes the role of raloxifene in osteoporosis management?', '["Raloxifene is a selective estrogen receptor modulator (SERM) that acts as an estrogen agonist on bone (reducing vertebral fracture risk) and an estrogen antagonist on breast tissue (reducing invasive breast cancer risk); it does NOT reduce hip fracture risk and increases the risk of venous thromboembolism and hot flashes", "Raloxifene is an estrogen agonist on both bone and breast tissue, increasing breast cancer risk", "Raloxifene reduces both vertebral and hip fracture risk equally and has no effect on breast cancer incidence", "Raloxifene acts as an estrogen antagonist on bone, reducing bone density, and is used solely for breast cancer chemoprevention", "Raloxifene is the first-line agent for osteoporosis in all postmenopausal women, regardless of breast cancer history"]'::jsonb, 0, '**Raloxifene** is a **selective estrogen receptor modulator (SERM)** with tissue-specific effects: (1) **Estrogen agonist on bone** - reduces bone resorption and decreases the risk of **vertebral fractures by ~30%**. (2) **Estrogen antagonist on breast tissue** - reduces the risk of **invasive ER-positive breast cancer by ~65%**. (3) **Does NOT reduce hip or non-vertebral fracture risk** (unlike bisphosphonates, denosumab, or teriparatide). **Adverse effects** include increased risk of **venous thromboembolism (DVT/PE)**, **hot flashes**, and **leg cramps**. Raloxifene is NOT first-line for osteoporosis (bisphosphonates are superior) but is a reasonable option for postmenopausal women at high breast cancer risk who also have osteoporosis, provided they have no history or risk factors for VTE. It is **contraindicated** in women with active or past VTE. (Ref: MORE Trial, JAMA 1999; Hazzard''s Ch. 77; First Aid Step 2 CK)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 69-year-old postmenopausal woman with very severe osteoporosis (T-score -4.0 at the femoral neck) and a recent vertebral compression fracture is being considered for romosozumab. She has no history of cardiovascular disease, myocardial infarction, or stroke. Which of the following best describes the mechanism and clinical considerations for romosozumab?', '["Romosozumab is a monoclonal antibody against sclerostin that has a dual mechanism: it stimulates bone formation (anabolic) and reduces bone resorption (anti-resorptive); it carries a boxed warning for increased cardiovascular risk (MI, stroke) and is limited to 12 monthly doses", "Romosozumab is a bisphosphonate with enhanced potency that can be used indefinitely without cardiovascular risk", "Romosozumab inhibits RANKL identical to denosumab and has no anabolic properties", "Romosozumab is a calcitonin analog that only reduces bone resorption without any effect on bone formation", "Romosozumab is administered as a once-yearly IV infusion like zoledronic acid and has no treatment duration limits"]'::jsonb, 0, '**Romosozumab** is a **humanized monoclonal antibody against sclerostin**, a glycoprotein produced by osteocytes that inhibits the Wnt signaling pathway. By blocking sclerostin, romosozumab has a **unique dual mechanism**: (1) **Stimulates bone formation** (anabolic effect via Wnt pathway activation of osteoblasts) AND (2) **Reduces bone resorption** (anti-resorptive effect). It is administered as **210 mg SC monthly for 12 months** (one course only). The **ARCH trial** showed romosozumab followed by alendronate reduced vertebral fracture risk by 48% vs. alendronate alone. **Key safety concern**: Romosozumab carries a **FDA boxed warning for cardiovascular risk** (increased risk of myocardial infarction, stroke, and cardiovascular death). It is **contraindicated** in patients with a history of MI or stroke within the preceding year. After completing 12 months, patients must be transitioned to a bisphosphonate or denosumab. (Ref: ARCH Trial, NEJM 2017; FDA Label 2019; Hazzard''s Ch. 77)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 93-year-old postmenopausal woman with very severe osteoporosis (T-score -4.0 at the femoral neck) and a recent vertebral compression fracture is being considered for romosozumab. She has no history of cardiovascular disease, myocardial infarction, or stroke. Which of the following best describes the mechanism and clinical considerations for romosozumab?', '["Romosozumab is administered as a once-yearly IV infusion like zoledronic acid and has no treatment duration limits", "Romosozumab inhibits RANKL identical to denosumab and has no anabolic properties", "Romosozumab is a monoclonal antibody against sclerostin that has a dual mechanism: it stimulates bone formation (anabolic) and reduces bone resorption (anti-resorptive); it carries a boxed warning for increased cardiovascular risk (MI, stroke) and is limited to 12 monthly doses", "Romosozumab is a calcitonin analog that only reduces bone resorption without any effect on bone formation", "Romosozumab is a bisphosphonate with enhanced potency that can be used indefinitely without cardiovascular risk"]'::jsonb, 2, '**Romosozumab** is a **humanized monoclonal antibody against sclerostin**, a glycoprotein produced by osteocytes that inhibits the Wnt signaling pathway. By blocking sclerostin, romosozumab has a **unique dual mechanism**: (1) **Stimulates bone formation** (anabolic effect via Wnt pathway activation of osteoblasts) AND (2) **Reduces bone resorption** (anti-resorptive effect). It is administered as **210 mg SC monthly for 12 months** (one course only). The **ARCH trial** showed romosozumab followed by alendronate reduced vertebral fracture risk by 48% vs. alendronate alone. **Key safety concern**: Romosozumab carries a **FDA boxed warning for cardiovascular risk** (increased risk of myocardial infarction, stroke, and cardiovascular death). It is **contraindicated** in patients with a history of MI or stroke within the preceding year. After completing 12 months, patients must be transitioned to a bisphosphonate or denosumab. (Ref: ARCH Trial, NEJM 2017; FDA Label 2019; Hazzard''s Ch. 77)'),
