    (v_topic_id, 'A 71-year-old postmenopausal female with very severe osteoporosis (T-score -3.8 at the femoral neck) and a recent vertebral compression fracture is being considered for romosozumab. She has no history of cardiovascular disease, myocardial infarction, or stroke. Which of the following best describes the mechanism and clinical considerations for romosozumab?', '["Romosozumab is a calcitonin analog that only reduces bone resorption without any effect on bone formation", "Romosozumab is administered as a once-yearly IV infusion like zoledronic acid and has no treatment duration limits", "Romosozumab is a monoclonal antibody against sclerostin that has a dual mechanism: it stimulates bone formation (anabolic) and reduces bone resorption (anti-resorptive); it carries a boxed warning for increased cardiovascular risk (MI, stroke) and is limited to 12 monthly doses", "Romosozumab inhibits RANKL identical to denosumab and has no anabolic properties", "Romosozumab is a bisphosphonate with enhanced potency that can be used indefinitely without cardiovascular risk"]'::jsonb, 2, '**Romosozumab** is a **humanized monoclonal antibody against sclerostin**, a glycoprotein produced by osteocytes that inhibits the Wnt signaling pathway. By blocking sclerostin, romosozumab has a **unique dual mechanism**: (1) **Stimulates bone formation** (anabolic effect via Wnt pathway activation of osteoblasts) AND (2) **Reduces bone resorption** (anti-resorptive effect). It is administered as **210 mg SC monthly for 12 months** (one course only). The **ARCH trial** showed romosozumab followed by alendronate reduced vertebral fracture risk by 48% vs. alendronate alone. **Key safety concern**: Romosozumab carries a **FDA boxed warning for cardiovascular risk** (increased risk of myocardial infarction, stroke, and cardiovascular death). It is **contraindicated** in patients with a history of MI or stroke within the preceding year. After completing 12 months, patients must be transiti