    (v_topic_id, 'A 78-year-old postmenopausal woman with very severe osteoporosis (T-score -3.5 at the femoral neck) and a recent vertebral compression fracture is being considered for romosozumab. She has no history of cardiovascular disease, myocardial infarction, or stroke. Which of the following best describes the mechanism and clinical considerations for romosozumab?', '["Romosozumab is a monoclonal antibody against sclerostin that has a dual mechanism: it stimulates bone formation (anabolic) and reduces bone resorption (anti-resorptive); it carries a boxed warning for increased cardiovascular risk (MI, stroke) and is limited to 12 monthly doses", "Romosozumab is a bisphosphonate with enhanced potency that can be used indefinitely without cardiovascular risk", "Romosozumab is administered as a once-yearly IV infusion like zoledronic acid and has no treatment duration limits", "Romosozumab is a calcitonin analog that only reduces bone resorption without any effect on bone formation", "Romosozumab inhibits RANKL identical to denosumab and has no anabolic properties"]'::jsonb, 0, '**Romosozumab** is a **humanized monoclonal antibody against sclerostin**, a glycoprotein produced by osteocytes that inhibits the Wnt signaling pathway. By blocking sclerostin, romosozumab has a **unique dual mechanism**: (1) **Stimulates bone formation** (anabolic effect via Wnt pathway activation of osteoblasts) AND (2) **Reduces bone resorption** (anti-resorptive effect). It is administered as **210 mg SC monthly for 12 months** (one course only). The **ARCH trial** showed romosozumab followed by alendronate reduced vertebral fracture risk by 48% vs. alendronate alone. **Key safety concern**: Romosozumab carries a **FDA boxed warning for cardiovascular risk** (increased risk of myocardial infarction, stroke, and cardiovascular death). It is **contraindicated** in patients with a history of MI or stroke within the preceding year. After completing 12 months, patients must be transitioned to a bisphosphonate or denosumab. (Ref: ARCH Trial, NEJM 2017; FDA Label 2019; Hazzard''s Ch. 77)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 68-year-old postmenopausal woman with very severe osteoporosis (T-score -3.2 at the femoral neck) and a recent vertebral compression fracture is being considered for romosozumab. She has no history of cardiovascular disease, myocardial infarction, or stroke. Which of the following best describes the mechanism and clinical considerations for romosozumab?', '["Romosozumab inhibits RANKL identical to denosumab and has no anabolic properties", "Romosozumab is a bisphosphonate with enhanced potency that can be used indefinitely without cardiovascular risk", "Romosozumab is administered as a once-yearly IV infusion like zoledronic acid and has no treatment duration limits", "Romosozumab is a calcitonin analog that only reduces bone resorption without any effect on bone formation", "Romosozumab is a monoclonal antibody against sclerostin that has a dual mechanism: it stimulates bone formation (anabolic) and reduces bone resorption (anti-resorptive); it carries a boxed warning for increased cardiovascular risk (MI, stroke) and is limited to 12 monthly doses"]'::jsonb, 4, '**Romosozumab** is a **humanized monoclonal antibody against sclerostin**, a glycoprotein produced by osteocytes that inhibits the Wnt signaling pathway. By blocking sclerostin, romosozumab has a **unique dual mechanism**: (1) **Stimulates bone formation** (anabolic effect via Wnt pathway activation of osteoblasts) AND (2) **Reduces bone resorption** (anti-resorptive effect). It is administered as **210 mg SC monthly for 12 months** (one course only). The **ARCH trial** showed romosozumab followed by alendronate reduced vertebral fracture risk by 48% vs. alendronate alone. **Key safety concern**: Romosozumab carries a **FDA boxed warning for cardiovascular risk** (increased risk of myocardial infarction, stroke, and cardiovascular death). It is **contraindicated** in patients with a history of MI or stroke within the preceding year. After completing 12 months, patients must be transitioned to a bisphosphonate or denosumab. (Ref: ARCH Trial, NEJM 2017; FDA Label 2019; Hazzard''s Ch. 77)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 90-year-old postmenopausal female with very severe osteoporosis (T-score -3.8 at the femoral neck) and a recent vertebral compression fracture is being considered for romosozumab. She has no history of cardiovascular disease, myocardial infarction, or stroke. Which of the following best describes the mechanism and clinical considerations for romosozumab?', '["Romosozumab is a calcitonin analog that only reduces bone resorption without any effect on bone formation", "Romosozumab is a monoclonal antibody against sclerostin that has a dual mechanism: it stimulates bone formation (anabolic) and reduces bone resorption (anti-resorptive); it carries a boxed warning for increased cardiovascular risk (MI, stroke) and is limited to 12 monthly doses", "Romosozumab is administered as a once-yearly IV infusion like zoledronic acid and has no treatment duration limits", "Romosozumab inhibits RANKL identical to denosumab and has no anabolic properties", "Romosozumab is a bisphosphonate with enhanced potency that can be used indefinitely without cardiovascular risk"]'::jsonb, 1, '**Romosozumab** is a **humanized monoclonal antibody against sclerostin**, a glycoprotein produced by osteocytes that inhibits the Wnt signaling pathway. By blocking sclerostin, romosozumab has a **unique dual mechanism**: (1) **Stimulates bone formation** (anabolic effect via Wnt pathway activation of osteoblasts) AND (2) **Reduces bone resorption** (anti-resorptive effect). It is administered as **210 mg SC monthly for 12 months** (one course only). The **ARCH trial** showed romosozumab followed by alendronate reduced vertebral fracture risk by 48% vs. alendronate alone. **Key safety concern**: Romosozumab carries a **FDA boxed warning for cardiovascular risk** (increased risk of myocardial infarction, stroke, and cardiovascular death). It is **contraindicated** in patients with a history of MI or stroke within the preceding year. After completing 12 months, patients must be transitioned to a bisphosphonate or denosumab. (Ref: ARCH Trial, NEJM 2017; FDA Label 2019; Hazzard''s Ch. 77)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 67-year-old female with rheumatoid arthritis has been started on prednisone 5 mg daily, which is expected to continue for at least 3 months. She has no prior fractures and her baseline DEXA T-score is -2.0. According to the American College of Rheumatology (ACR) guidelines, which of the following is the most appropriate approach to prevent glucocorticoid-induced osteoporosis (GIOP)?', '["Start calcium (1000-1200 mg/day) and vitamin D (800-1000 IU/day) for all patients on glucocorticoids; with a T-score of -2.0 and prednisone >= 2.5 mg/day for >= 3 months, add bisphosphonate prophylaxis based on fracture risk assessment using FRAX (with glucocorticoid adjustment)", "Defer any intervention until repeat DEXA in 5 years to monitor for bone loss progression", "No intervention is needed because prednisone at low doses protects against bone loss", "Only prescribe calcium supplementation without vitamin D because vitamin D is not affected by glucocorticoid use", "Start teriparatide immediately for all patients regardless of T-score and glucocorticoid dose"]'::jsonb, 0, '**Glucocorticoid-induced osteoporosis (GIOP)** is the most common form of **secondary osteoporosis**. Glucocorticoids cause bone loss through multiple mechanisms: (1) **Inhibiting osteoblast function and promoting osteoblast/osteocyte apoptosis** (primary mechanism), (2) Increasing osteoclast activity, (3) Reducing intestinal calcium absorption and increasing renal calcium excretion, and (4) Suppressing GnRH, causing hypogonadism. Bone loss is **most rapid in the first 3-6 months** of therapy. The **ACR 2017 GIOP guidelines** recommend: For adults >= 40 years on prednisone >= 2.5 mg/day for >= 3 months, **initiate prophylactic therapy** with oral bisphosphonates (first-line), or IV bisphosphonates, or teriparatide (for very high-risk patients). Calcium (1000-1200 mg/day) and vitamin D (600-800 IU/day) should be started for ALL patients. FRAX underestimates fracture risk in glucocorticoid users, so risk adjustment is needed. (Ref: ACR 2017 GIOP Guidelines; Hazzard''s Ch. 77; Current Geriatrics)'),
