    (v_topic_id, 'A 94-year-old female with rheumatoid arthritis has been started on prednisone 10 mg daily, which is expected to continue for at least 6 months. She has no prior fractures and her baseline DEXA T-score is -1.5. According to the American College of Rheumatology (ACR) guidelines, which of the following is the most appropriate approach to prevent glucocorticoid-induced osteoporosis (GIOP)?', '["Defer any intervention until repeat DEXA in 5 years to monitor for bone loss progression", "No intervention is needed because prednisone at low doses protects against bone loss", "Start calcium (1000-1200 mg/day) and vitamin D (800-1000 IU/day) for all patients on glucocorticoids; with a T-score of -1.5 and prednisone >= 2.5 mg/day for >= 3 months, add bisphosphonate prophylaxis based on fracture risk assessment using FRAX (with glucocorticoid adjustment)", "Start teriparatide immediately for all patients regardless of T-score and glucocorticoid dose", "Only prescribe calcium supplementation without vitamin D because vitamin D is not affected by glucocorticoid use"]'::jsonb, 2, '**Glucocorticoid-induced osteoporosis (GIOP)** is the most common form of **secondary osteoporosis**. Glucocorticoids cause bone loss through multiple mechanisms: (1) **Inhibiting osteoblast function and promoting osteoblast/osteocyte apoptosis** (primary mechanism), (2) Increasing osteoclast activity, (3) Reducing intestinal calcium absorption and increasing renal calcium excretion, and (4) Suppressing GnRH, causing hypogonadism. Bone loss is **most rapid in the first 3-6 months** of therapy. The **ACR 2017 GIOP guidelines** recommend: For adults >= 40 years on prednisone >= 2.5 mg/day for >= 3 months, **initiate prophylactic therapy** with oral bisphosphonates (first-line), or IV bisphosphonates, or teriparatide (for very high-risk patients). Calcium (1000-1200 mg/day) and vitamin D (600-800 IU/day) should be started for ALL patients. FRAX underestimates fracture risk in glucocorticoid