 users, so risk adjustment is needed. (Ref: ACR 2017 GIOP Guidelines; Hazzard''s Ch. 77; Current Geriatrics)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 79-year-old female with rheumatoid arthritis has been started on prednisone 10 mg daily, which is expected to continue for at least 12 months. She has no prior fractures and her baseline DEXA T-score is -2.3. According to the American College of Rheumatology (ACR) guidelines, which of the following is the most appropriate approach to prevent glucocorticoid-induced osteoporosis (GIOP)?', '["Start calcium (1000-1200 mg/day) and vitamin D (800-1000 IU/day) for all patients on glucocorticoids; with a T-score of -2.3 and prednisone >= 2.5 mg/day for >= 3 months, add bisphosphonate prophylaxis based on fracture risk assessment using FRAX (with glucocorticoid adjustment)", "No intervention is needed because prednisone at low doses protects against bone loss", "Start teriparatide immediately for all patients regardless of T-score and glucocorticoid dose", "Only prescribe calcium supplementation without vitamin D because vitamin D is not affected by glucocorticoid use", "Defer any intervention until repeat DEXA in 5 years to monitor for bone loss progression"]'::jsonb, 0, '**Glucocorticoid-induced osteoporosis (GIOP)** is the most common form of **secondary osteoporosis**. Glucocorticoids cause bone loss through multiple mechanisms: (1) **Inhibiting osteoblast function and promoting osteoblast/osteocyte apoptosis** (primary mechanism), (2) Increasing osteoclast activity, (3) Reducing intestinal calcium absorption and increasing renal calcium excretion, and (4) Suppressing GnRH, causing hypogonadism. Bone loss is **most rapid in the first 3-6 months** of therapy. The **ACR 2017 GIOP guidelines** recommend: For adults >= 40 years on prednisone >= 2.5 mg/day for >= 3 months, **initiate prophylactic therapy** with oral bisphosphonates (first-line), or IV bisphosphonates, or teriparatide (for very high-risk patients). Calcium (1000-1200 mg/day) and vitamin D (600-800 IU/day) should be started for ALL patients. FRAX underestimates fracture risk in glucocorticoid users, so risk adjustment is needed. (Ref: ACR 2017 GIOP Guidelines; Hazzard''s Ch. 77; Current Geriatrics)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 76-year-old postmenopausal female with a recent acute vertebral compression fracture presents with severe back pain. Intranasal calcitonin is being considered. Which of the following best describes the current role of calcitonin in osteoporosis management?', '["Calcitonin has no role in osteoporosis management and has been removed from all clinical guidelines", "Calcitonin is the preferred agent for glucocorticoid-induced osteoporosis prevention because it directly counteracts prednisone''s mechanism", "Calcitonin is the most effective anti-resorptive agent available and is first-line for all patients with osteoporosis", "Calcitonin is an anabolic agent that stimulates osteoblast activity and new bone formation", "Calcitonin has a modest anti-resorptive effect and a potential analgesic effect for acute vertebral fracture pain; however, it is NOT first-line for osteoporosis because its fracture reduction efficacy is inferior to bisphosphonates, denosumab, and teriparatide, and it carries a signal for increased malignancy risk with prolonged use"]'::jsonb, 4, '**Calcitonin** (salmon calcitonin, available as intranasal spray or subcutaneous injection) has a **modest anti-resorptive effect** by directly inhibiting osteoclast activity. Its BMD-increasing effect is significantly weaker than bisphosphonates, denosumab, or teriparatide. Calcitonin''s primary niche is its **potential analgesic effect** for **acute pain from vertebral compression fractures** (mechanism not fully understood; possibly related to beta-endorphin release). However, it is **NOT first-line** for osteoporosis treatment or prevention due to: (1) Inferior fracture reduction efficacy, (2) An FDA safety signal for a possible **increased risk of malignancy** (prostate cancer, hepatocellular cancer) with prolonged use (>6 months), prompting the EMA to withdraw it from the European market. Current guidelines limit its use to short-term pain management in acute vertebral fractures when other analgesics are insufficient. (Ref: PROOF Trial, AJM 2000; FDA Safety Review 2013; Hazzard''s Ch. 77)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 70-year-old postmenopausal female with a recent acute vertebral compression fracture presents with severe back pain. Intranasal calcitonin is being considered. Which of the following best describes the current role of calcitonin in osteoporosis management?', '["Calcitonin is an anabolic agent that stimulates osteoblast activity and new bone formation", "Calcitonin has a modest anti-resorptive effect and a potential analgesic effect for acute vertebral fracture pain; however, it is NOT first-line for osteoporosis because its fracture reduction efficacy is inferior to bisphosphonates, denosumab, and teriparatide, and it carries a signal for increased malignancy risk with prolonged use", "Calcitonin has no role in osteoporosis management and has been removed from all clinical guidelines", "Calcitonin is the preferred agent for glucocorticoid-induced osteoporosis prevention because it directly counteracts prednisone''s mechanism", "Calcitonin is the most effective anti-resorptive agent available and is first-line for all patients with osteoporosis"]'::jsonb, 1, '**Calcitonin** (salmon calcitonin, available as intranasal spray or subcutaneous injection) has a **modest anti-resorptive effect** by directly inhibiting osteoclast activity. Its BMD-increasing effect is significantly weaker than bisphosphonates, denosumab, or teriparatide. Calcitonin''s primary niche is its **potential analgesic effect** for **acute pain from vertebral compression fractures** (mechanism not fully understood; possibly related to beta-endorphin release). However, it is **NOT first-line** for osteoporosis treatment or prevention due to: (1) Inferior fracture reduction efficacy, (2) An FDA safety signal for a possible **increased risk of malignancy** (prostate cancer, hepatocellular cancer) with prolonged use (>6 months), prompting the EMA to withdraw it from the European market. Current guidelines limit its use to short-term pain management in acute vertebral fractures when other analgesics are insufficient. (Ref: PROOF Trial, AJM 2000; FDA Safety Review 2013; Hazzard''s Ch. 77)'),
